The p-value of approximately 0.0219 is less than the significance level of 0.05. Therefore, the null hypothesis is rejected. The experiment provides statistically significant evidence of a difference in average execution time between the two methods. The 95% confidence interval for the difference in means is entirely positive. This supports the conclusion that Option 2 takes longer on average than Option 1 under the conditions represented by these measurements.
Conclusion
This experiment compared two multithreaded methods of validating a Sudoku board using 50 recorded execution times per method. The null hypothesis stated that there was no statistically significant difference in the average execution times.
The calculated mean execution times were 0.000938 seconds for Option 1 and 0.001256 seconds for Option 2. Welch's t-test produced a p-value of approximately 0.0219, which is below the 0.05 significance level. Consequently, the null hypothesis was rejected.
The experimental results indicate that Option 1 was statistically faster than Option 2 for the recorded runs. This suggests that creating additional threads does not necessarily improve performance when the tasks are small and thread-management overhead is significant.


